          "heading": "Process Simulation & Industrial Design Case: Flowsheets, Specifications and Degree-of-Freedom Accounting",
          "content": "# Unit 1 applied simulation project \u2014 Flowsheets, Specifications and Conservation\n\n**Case:** A blending and splitting train that combines two soluble streams and routes product around an optional bypass.\n\n## Engineering brief\n\nPrepare an auditable model that can answer a defined question related to make a reproducible flowsheet from a physical question: boundaries, typed streams, independent variables, specifications and closure. The main deliverable is not a large software project; it is a compact chain of engineering reasoning supported by one or more simulation results and at least one hand-checkable benchmark. Adopt an explicitly synthetic set of operating inputs unless verified experimental data are supplied.\n\n## Model specification\n\nUse a coherent subset of these relationships: `sum of component input = sum of component output + accumulation`, `F_out z_A,out = F_1 z_A,1 + F_2 z_A,2`, `DOF = number of independent unknowns \u2212 number of independent equations` and `relative residual = (inputs \u2212 outputs + generation \u2212 accumulation) / reference throughput`. Select only relations required by your declared boundary. Describe the balance basis and state or flow variables, and include a table distinguishing imposed specifications from computed stream properties. Confirm that the system is neither underspecified nor contradictory.\n\n## Proposed investigation sequence\n\n**A \u2014 Baseline:** Draw the mixer boundary, mark three independent composition variables and determine whether the flow specification fully identifies an outlet. Record the starting parameters and the output. Reproduce one relevant calculation without the software and check its units.\n\n**B \u2014 Physical sensitivity:** Change the product split fraction and verify both branch A flows sum to the mixer A feed within floating-point tolerance. Select the parameter alteration before observing the outcome, then explain the sign and approximate magnitude using the governing relation.\n\n**C \u2014 Numerical or model stress:** Write the unknown/equation list for a two-feed splitter including one unsupplied outlet temperature; explain what thermal information is missing. Find a range limit, solver issue or omitted effect, and describe why it matters. Preserve evidence of nonconvergence if observed.\n\n**D \u2014 Decision test:** Construct a verification sheet listing the expected total and component closure for five deliberately perturbed mixer cases. Write a recommendation conditional on the mathematical assumptions and any unresolved physical constraints.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\nA reviewer must be able to identify where every numeric input came from, rerun the model offline, understand at least two output curves, see a component or energy balance residual, and read a limitation statement relevant to the chosen process. Provide both ordinary operating and stress cases. Compare your conclusions with what would change if a rate coefficient, property choice, equipment geometry or feed state were uncertain.\n\n## Reporting format\n\nSubmit a concise technical memo containing: process objective; stream and parameter tables; equations; one worked benchmark; labeled model plots; CSV appendix; a verification checklist; a short uncertainty or sensitivity analysis; and a recommendation marked explicitly as an educational screening result. Do not call it a validated industrial design.\n\n[Unit 1 teaching material](../units/unit-01.html) \u00b7 [Unit assessment](../assessments/unit-01.html)"
