          "content": "# Unit 1 assessment \u2014 Flowsheets, Specifications and Conservation\n\n**Assessment design:** 16 substantive problems, four for each lesson. These are not auto-scored trivia questions. Show equations, units, actual calculation steps, error checks and model validity statements. Do not present the synthetic values as physical measurements.\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1: Formulation \u2014 Defining the system boundary and stream table.** Begin with the physical equipment boundary and derive or justify `sum of component input = sum of component output + accumulation`. Define each variable, its physical units and its status as an input, state or model output. Name one plausible extra process effect requiring an additional equation.\n\n**Q1: Independent arithmetic \u2014 Defining the system boundary and stream table.** Work from the following synthetic reference case without copying the plotted output: A mixer receives 2 kmol/h of A and 3 kmol/h of B; the product total is 5 kmol/h if there are no losses. Show substitutions and check an appropriate conservation or limit.\n\n**Q1: Model challenge \u2014 Defining the system boundary and stream table.** Draw the mixer boundary, mark three independent composition variables and determine whether the flow specification fully identifies an outlet. Explain what quantitative outcome would support the model and what result would contradict it.\n\n**Q1: Responsible interpretation \u2014 Defining the system boundary and stream table.** The model has this restriction: An undocumented evaporation loss invalidates the \u201cno generation\u201d statement even when the software converges. Propose one measured variable or external data source that would be needed before a real engineering application.\n\n**Q5: Formulation \u2014 Component and total material balances.** Begin with the physical equipment boundary and derive or justify `F_out z_A,out = F_1 z_A,1 + F_2 z_A,2`. Define each variable, its physical units and its status as an input, state or model output. Name one plausible extra process effect requiring an additional equation.\n\n**Q5: Independent arithmetic \u2014 Component and total material balances.** Work from the following synthetic reference case without copying the plotted output: With 4 kmol/h at x_A=0.20 and 6 kmol/h at x_A=0.70, A leaves at 5.0 kmol/h and the mixed mole fraction is 0.50. Show substitutions and check an appropriate conservation or limit.\n\n**Q5: Model challenge \u2014 Component and total material balances.** Change the product split fraction and verify both branch A flows sum to the mixer A feed within floating-point tolerance. Explain what quantitative outcome would support the model and what result would contradict it.\n\n**Q5: Responsible interpretation \u2014 Component and total material balances.** The model has this restriction: In a flashing or reacting mixer, an inert total-flow balance may remain true while the naive component relation must include phases or reaction sources. Propose one measured variable or external data source that would be needed before a real engineering application.\n\n**Q9: Formulation \u2014 Degrees of freedom and specification logic.** Begin with the physical equipment boundary and derive or justify `DOF = number of independent unknowns \u2212 number of independent equations`. Define each variable, its physical units and its status as an input, state or model output. Name one plausible extra process effect requiring an additional equation.\n\n**Q9: Independent arithmetic \u2014 Degrees of freedom and specification logic.** Work from the following synthetic reference case without copying the plotted output: A binary ideal mixer with fixed inlet flow and composition has two unknown outlet component rates and two independent component balances: DOF=0. Show substitutions and check an appropriate conservation or limit.\n\n**Q9: Model challenge \u2014 Degrees of freedom and specification logic.** Write the unknown/equation list for a two-feed splitter including one unsupplied outlet temperature; explain what thermal information is missing. Explain what quantitative outcome would support the model and what result would contradict it.\n\n**Q9: Responsible interpretation \u2014 Degrees of freedom and specification logic.** The model has this restriction: Two duplicate mass balances do not yield two independent constraints. A solver can report success despite a poorly conditioned redundant system. Propose one measured variable or external data source that would be needed before a real engineering application.\n\n**Q13: Formulation \u2014 Flowsheet documentation and balance audits.** Begin with the physical equipment boundary and derive or justify `relative residual = (inputs \u2212 outputs + generation \u2212 accumulation) / reference throughput`. Define each variable, its physical units and its status as an input, state or model output. Name one plausible extra process effect requiring an additional equation.\n\n**Q13: Independent arithmetic \u2014 Flowsheet documentation and balance audits.** Work from the following synthetic reference case without copying the plotted output: For a 100 kmol/h feed and 99.98 kmol/h reported products, closure error is 0.02%, not automatically acceptable without a declared tolerance. Show substitutions and check an appropriate conservation or limit.\n\n**Q13: Model challenge \u2014 Flowsheet documentation and balance audits.** Construct a verification sheet listing the expected total and component closure for five deliberately perturbed mixer cases. Explain what quantitative outcome would support the model and what result would contradict it.\n\n**Q13: Responsible interpretation \u2014 Flowsheet documentation and balance audits.** The model has this restriction: Dividing by an almost-zero reference flow produces misleading normalized residuals; use an absolute tolerance for small streams. Propose one measured variable or external data source that would be needed before a real engineering application.\n\n## Required deliverables\n\nHand in a stream table or variable table, four independent calculations, one CSV export from a relevant laboratory, at least two numerical residuals and a short paragraph explaining which uncertainty would most influence the engineering choice. A technically correct calculation without clear assumptions and units is incomplete.\n\n## Assessment rubric (20 points)\n\n- Conservation-law and constitutive-model formulation: 5 points.\n\n- Correct numerical work, correct units and transparent assumptions: 5 points.\n\n- Independent numerical or limiting-case verification: 5 points.\n\n- Careful interpretation, validity range and communication: 5 points.\n\n[Unit lessons](../units/unit-01.html) \u00b7 [Applied project](../projects/unit-01.html)"
