          "id": "sec-2-7",
          "secNumber": "2.7",
          "heading": "Engineering Audit, Diagnostic Reasoning & Assessment: Thermodynamic Property Packages and Phase Equilibria",
          "content": "# Unit 2 assessment \u2014 Property Models and Thermodynamic Equilibrium\n\n**Assessment design:** 16 substantive problems, four for each lesson. These are not auto-scored trivia questions. Show equations, units, actual calculation steps, error checks and model validity statements. Do not present the synthetic values as physical measurements.\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1: Formulation \u2014 Property packages and reference states.** Begin with the physical equipment boundary and derive or justify `h(T) \u2212 h(T_ref) = integral(Cp(T), T_ref to T)`. Define each variable, its physical units and its status as an input, state or model output. Name one plausible extra process effect requiring an additional equation.\n\n**Q1: Independent arithmetic \u2014 Property packages and reference states.** Work from the following synthetic reference case without copying the plotted output: For Cp=4 kJ/(kg K), a 5 kg/s single-phase stream heated by 20 K requires 400 kW of sensible heat. Show substitutions and check an appropriate conservation or limit.\n\n**Q1: Model challenge \u2014 Property packages and reference states.** Compute the effect of a \u00b110% Cp uncertainty on a heater duty and explain why the outlet temperature alone does not reveal reference-state consistency. Explain what quantitative outcome would support the model and what result would contradict it.\n\n**Q1: Responsible interpretation \u2014 Property packages and reference states.** The model has this restriction: Constant heat capacity does not describe vaporization, strong temperature dependence or a reacting mixture across a wide range. Propose one measured variable or external data source that would be needed before a real engineering application.\n\n**Q5: Formulation \u2014 Antoine vapor pressure and Raoult-law bubble point.** Begin with the physical equipment boundary and derive or justify `P = x_ethanol P_sat,ethanol(T) + (1\u2212x_ethanol) P_sat,water(T)`. Define each variable, its physical units and its status as an input, state or model output. Name one plausible extra process effect requiring an additional equation.\n\n**Q5: Independent arithmetic \u2014 Antoine vapor pressure and Raoult-law bubble point.** Work from the following synthetic reference case without copying the plotted output: At a specified liquid ethanol fraction, the bubble temperature is the root of summed ideal partial pressures minus total pressure. Show substitutions and check an appropriate conservation or limit.\n\n**Q5: Model challenge \u2014 Antoine vapor pressure and Raoult-law bubble point.** Find the bubble point at two ethanol fractions and verify that the calculated vapor fraction of ethanol differs from the liquid fraction. Explain what quantitative outcome would support the model and what result would contradict it.\n\n**Q5: Responsible interpretation \u2014 Antoine vapor pressure and Raoult-law bubble point.** The model has this restriction: Ethanol/water is nonideal in important regimes; the ideal calculation is a pedagogical benchmark, not a rigorous azeotrope model. Propose one measured variable or external data source that would be needed before a real engineering application.\n\n**Q9: Formulation \u2014 Isothermal flash and Rachford\u2013Rice logic.** Begin with the physical equipment boundary and derive or justify `sum_i z_i (K_i\u22121)/(1+beta (K_i\u22121)) = 0`. Define each variable, its physical units and its status as an input, state or model output. Name one plausible extra process effect requiring an additional equation.\n\n**Q9: Independent arithmetic \u2014 Isothermal flash and Rachford\u2013Rice logic.** Work from the following synthetic reference case without copying the plotted output: For z_A=0.5, K_A=2 and K_B=0.5, the two-phase solution is beta=0.5, x_A=1/3, y_A=2/3. Show substitutions and check an appropriate conservation or limit.\n\n**Q9: Model challenge \u2014 Isothermal flash and Rachford\u2013Rice logic.** At a selected feed composition, recompute both phase fractions and the A component balance from the chart data. Explain what quantitative outcome would support the model and what result would contradict it.\n\n**Q9: Responsible interpretation \u2014 Isothermal flash and Rachford\u2013Rice logic.** The model has this restriction: Constant arbitrary K values neglect temperature, pressure and mixture dependence; two-phase roots are not evidence that the chosen property package is appropriate. Propose one measured variable or external data source that would be needed before a real engineering application.\n\n**Q13: Formulation \u2014 Chemical equilibrium versus phase equilibrium.** Begin with the physical equipment boundary and derive or justify `K_eq(T) = K_ref exp[\u2212\u0394H/R (1/T \u2212 1/T_ref)]`. Define each variable, its physical units and its status as an input, state or model output. Name one plausible extra process effect requiring an additional equation.\n\n**Q13: Independent arithmetic \u2014 Chemical equilibrium versus phase equilibrium.** Work from the following synthetic reference case without copying the plotted output: For A \u21cc B with ideal activities and K_eq=4, x_B at equilibrium is K/(1+K)=0.80. Show substitutions and check an appropriate conservation or limit.\n\n**Q13: Model challenge \u2014 Chemical equilibrium versus phase equilibrium.** Switch \u0394H from negative to positive and interpret the reversal in the conversion\u2013temperature slope. Explain what quantitative outcome would support the model and what result would contradict it.\n\n**Q13: Responsible interpretation \u2014 Chemical equilibrium versus phase equilibrium.** The model has this restriction: Constant reaction enthalpy and ideal activities are modeling choices, not universal descriptions of real reaction chemistry. Propose one measured variable or external data source that would be needed before a real engineering application.\n\n## Required deliverables\n\nHand in a stream table or variable table, four independent calculations, one CSV export from a relevant laboratory, at least two numerical residuals and a short paragraph explaining which uncertainty would most influence the engineering choice. A technically correct calculation without clear assumptions and units is incomplete.\n\n## Assessment rubric (20 points)\n\n- Conservation-law and constitutive-model formulation: 5 points.\n\n- Correct numerical work, correct units and transparent assumptions: 5 points.\n\n- Independent numerical or limiting-case verification: 5 points.\n\n- Careful interpretation, validity range and communication: 5 points.\n\n[Unit lessons](../units/unit-02.html) \u00b7 [Applied project](../projects/unit-02.html)"
