          "heading": "Engineering Audit, Diagnostic Reasoning & Assessment: Unit Operations and Rigorous Equipment Modeling",
          "content": "# Unit 3 assessment \u2014 Unit Operations and Equipment Models\n\n**Assessment design:** 16 substantive problems, four for each lesson. These are not auto-scored trivia questions. Show equations, units, actual calculation steps, error checks and model validity statements. Do not present the synthetic values as physical measurements.\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1: Formulation \u2014 Hydraulic system curves and operating points.** Begin with the physical equipment boundary and derive or justify `H_pump(Q)=H0\u2212aQ\u00b2; H_sys(Q)=KQ\u00b2`. Define each variable, its physical units and its status as an input, state or model output. Name one plausible extra process effect requiring an additional equation.\n\n**Q1: Independent arithmetic \u2014 Hydraulic system curves and operating points.** Work from the following synthetic reference case without copying the plotted output: With H0=40 m, a=1000 s\u00b2/m\u2075 and K=1500 s\u00b2/m\u2075, the operating rate is sqrt(40/2500)\u22480.1265 m\u00b3/s. Show substitutions and check an appropriate conservation or limit.\n\n**Q1: Model challenge \u2014 Hydraulic system curves and operating points.** Increase system resistance by 25%; independently compute the new intersection and compare with the simulator metric. Explain what quantitative outcome would support the model and what result would contradict it.\n\n**Q1: Responsible interpretation \u2014 Hydraulic system curves and operating points.** The model has this restriction: Pump affinity rules are approximate, and the curves here are hypothetical; they are not a vendor rating or an NPSH assessment. Propose one measured variable or external data source that would be needed before a real engineering application.\n\n**Q5: Formulation \u2014 Heat-exchanger effectiveness and capacity rates.** Begin with the physical equipment boundary and derive or justify `Q=epsilon C_min (T_hot,in \u2212 T_cold,in)`. Define each variable, its physical units and its status as an input, state or model output. Name one plausible extra process effect requiring an additional equation.\n\n**Q5: Independent arithmetic \u2014 Heat-exchanger effectiveness and capacity rates.** Work from the following synthetic reference case without copying the plotted output: For C_h=3 kW/K, C_c=2 kW/K and delta T_in=50 K, the thermodynamic upper bound is Q_max=100 kW. Show substitutions and check an appropriate conservation or limit.\n\n**Q5: Model challenge \u2014 Heat-exchanger effectiveness and capacity rates.** Double UA and report how duty and both outlet temperatures change; explain why a doubling of UA cannot double duty indefinitely. Explain what quantitative outcome would support the model and what result would contradict it.\n\n**Q5: Responsible interpretation \u2014 Heat-exchanger effectiveness and capacity rates.** The model has this restriction: The analytic constant-Cp expression does not predict phase change, fouling evolution, or temperature-dependent UA. Propose one measured variable or external data source that would be needed before a real engineering application.\n\n**Q9: Formulation \u2014 Heaters, duties and utility feasibility.** Begin with the physical equipment boundary and derive or justify `Q=m_dot Cp (T_target\u2212T_in)`. Define each variable, its physical units and its status as an input, state or model output. Name one plausible extra process effect requiring an additional equation.\n\n**Q9: Independent arithmetic \u2014 Heaters, duties and utility feasibility.** Work from the following synthetic reference case without copying the plotted output: Heating 2.5 kg/s at 3.8 kJ/(kg K) by 40 K requires 380 kW. Show substitutions and check an appropriate conservation or limit.\n\n**Q9: Model challenge \u2014 Heaters, duties and utility feasibility.** Vary inlet flow and record the proportional change in duty; then increase target temperature and explain why this two-parameter effect is not always a simple scale factor. Explain what quantitative outcome would support the model and what result would contradict it.\n\n**Q9: Responsible interpretation \u2014 Heaters, duties and utility feasibility.** The model has this restriction: A single-phase constant-Cp estimate is invalid when evaporation, condensation or chemical heat occurs inside the heater. Propose one measured variable or external data source that would be needed before a real engineering application.\n\n**Q13: Formulation \u2014 Pressure, energy and model-coupling constraints.** Begin with the physical equipment boundary and derive or justify `P_out = P_in \u2212 \u0394P; H_out = H_in + Q \u2212 W`. Define each variable, its physical units and its status as an input, state or model output. Name one plausible extra process effect requiring an additional equation.\n\n**Q13: Independent arithmetic \u2014 Pressure, energy and model-coupling constraints.** Work from the following synthetic reference case without copying the plotted output: A liquid train with 4 bar feed, 1.2 bar pipe loss and 0.4 bar exchanger loss has 2.4 bar remaining before any downstream equipment. Show substitutions and check an appropriate conservation or limit.\n\n**Q13: Model challenge \u2014 Pressure, energy and model-coupling constraints.** Build a pressure budget for a pump\u2013exchanger\u2013valve train and identify the minimum pump discharge pressure needed to keep every intermediate pressure positive. Explain what quantitative outcome would support the model and what result would contradict it.\n\n**Q13: Responsible interpretation \u2014 Pressure, energy and model-coupling constraints.** The model has this restriction: Neglecting pressure loss changes equilibrium calculations, vapor fraction and mechanical duty, especially near a phase boundary. Propose one measured variable or external data source that would be needed before a real engineering application.\n\n## Required deliverables\n\nHand in a stream table or variable table, four independent calculations, one CSV export from a relevant laboratory, at least two numerical residuals and a short paragraph explaining which uncertainty would most influence the engineering choice. A technically correct calculation without clear assumptions and units is incomplete.\n\n## Assessment rubric (20 points)\n\n- Conservation-law and constitutive-model formulation: 5 points.\n\n- Correct numerical work, correct units and transparent assumptions: 5 points.\n\n- Independent numerical or limiting-case verification: 5 points.\n\n- Careful interpretation, validity range and communication: 5 points.\n\n[Unit lessons](../units/unit-03.html) \u00b7 [Applied project](../projects/unit-03.html)"
