          "heading": "Engineering Audit, Diagnostic Reasoning & Assessment: Flowsheet Recycle Loops and Numerical Convergence",
          "content": "# Unit 6 assessment \u2014 Recycles, Numerical Solvers and Convergence\n\n**Assessment design:** 16 substantive problems, four for each lesson. These are not auto-scored trivia questions. Show equations, units, actual calculation steps, error checks and model validity statements. Do not present the synthetic values as physical measurements.\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1: Formulation \u2014 Recycle and purge as component balances.** Begin with the physical equipment boundary and derive or justify `I_loop = I_fresh/[1\u2212r(1\u2212p)]`. Define each variable, its physical units and its status as an input, state or model output. Name one plausible extra process effect requiring an additional equation.\n\n**Q1: Independent arithmetic \u2014 Recycle and purge as component balances.** Work from the following synthetic reference case without copying the plotted output: An inert fresh input of 1 kmol/h, recycle fraction r=0.8 and purge fraction p=0.1 yield I_loop=1/0.28\u22483.571 kmol/h. Show substitutions and check an appropriate conservation or limit.\n\n**Q1: Model challenge \u2014 Recycle and purge as component balances.** Calculate the loop inventory for purge fractions 0, 0.05, 0.1 and 0.2 and explain the steepness near a closed loop. Explain what quantitative outcome would support the model and what result would contradict it.\n\n**Q1: Responsible interpretation \u2014 Recycle and purge as component balances.** The model has this restriction: This single-inert equation is not a model of reaction conversion, phase separation or pressure-driven recycle feasibility. Propose one measured variable or external data source that would be needed before a real engineering application.\n\n**Q5: Formulation \u2014 Tear streams and fixed-point convergence.** Begin with the physical equipment boundary and derive or justify `x_(n+1)=b+a x_n; x*=b/(1\u2212a)`. Define each variable, its physical units and its status as an input, state or model output. Name one plausible extra process effect requiring an additional equation.\n\n**Q5: Independent arithmetic \u2014 Tear streams and fixed-point convergence.** Work from the following synthetic reference case without copying the plotted output: With a=0.6 and b=4, the fixed point is 10; with a=1.05 the naive iteration diverges despite a formal fixed point of \u221280. Show substitutions and check an appropriate conservation or limit.\n\n**Q5: Model challenge \u2014 Tear streams and fixed-point convergence.** Compare a=0.4, 0.9 and 1.1; determine how the number of iterations required for a specified residual changes. Explain what quantitative outcome would support the model and what result would contradict it.\n\n**Q5: Responsible interpretation \u2014 Tear streams and fixed-point convergence.** The model has this restriction: Convergence of a scalar toy iteration does not establish convergence of a coupled nonlinear, multiphase recycle flowsheet. Propose one measured variable or external data source that would be needed before a real engineering application.\n\n**Q9: Formulation \u2014 Numerical error: Euler versus Runge\u2013Kutta.** Begin with the physical equipment boundary and derive or justify `dy/dt=\u2212k y; y_exact=exp(\u2212kt)`. Define each variable, its physical units and its status as an input, state or model output. Name one plausible extra process effect requiring an additional equation.\n\n**Q9: Independent arithmetic \u2014 Numerical error: Euler versus Runge\u2013Kutta.** Work from the following synthetic reference case without copying the plotted output: For k=1 s^-1 and step h=0.5 s, Euler gives y(1)=0.25, whereas exact is 0.3679; RK4 is much closer. Show substitutions and check an appropriate conservation or limit.\n\n**Q9: Model challenge \u2014 Numerical error: Euler versus Runge\u2013Kutta.** Halve the Euler and RK4 step and compare final-time error ratios; explain deviations caused by step-count rounding. Explain what quantitative outcome would support the model and what result would contradict it.\n\n**Q9: Responsible interpretation \u2014 Numerical error: Euler versus Runge\u2013Kutta.** The model has this restriction: Stiff kinetic systems often need implicit algorithms and event handling; the small offline demonstration is not a stiff solver. Propose one measured variable or external data source that would be needed before a real engineering application.\n\n**Q13: Formulation \u2014 Diagnosing oscillation, divergence and false closure.** Begin with the physical equipment boundary and derive or justify `relative change = |x_(n+1)\u2212x_n| / max(1,|x_n|)`. Define each variable, its physical units and its status as an input, state or model output. Name one plausible extra process effect requiring an additional equation.\n\n**Q13: Independent arithmetic \u2014 Diagnosing oscillation, divergence and false closure.** Work from the following synthetic reference case without copying the plotted output: An iteration at x_n=1000 and x_(n+1)=1000.01 has a small relative update yet may violate a 10 kmol/h component balance. Show substitutions and check an appropriate conservation or limit.\n\n**Q13: Model challenge \u2014 Diagnosing oscillation, divergence and false closure.** Describe a three-part diagnostic using iteration trend, balance residual and sensitivity to initial guess. Explain what quantitative outcome would support the model and what result would contradict it.\n\n**Q13: Responsible interpretation \u2014 Diagnosing oscillation, divergence and false closure.** The model has this restriction: A falsely converged recycle may produce believable energy consumption numbers while violating conservation upstream of the tear. Propose one measured variable or external data source that would be needed before a real engineering application.\n\n## Required deliverables\n\nHand in a stream table or variable table, four independent calculations, one CSV export from a relevant laboratory, at least two numerical residuals and a short paragraph explaining which uncertainty would most influence the engineering choice. A technically correct calculation without clear assumptions and units is incomplete.\n\n## Assessment rubric (20 points)\n\n- Conservation-law and constitutive-model formulation: 5 points.\n\n- Correct numerical work, correct units and transparent assumptions: 5 points.\n\n- Independent numerical or limiting-case verification: 5 points.\n\n- Careful interpretation, validity range and communication: 5 points.\n\n[Unit lessons](../units/unit-06.html) \u00b7 [Applied project](../projects/unit-06.html)"
